#  webkit-wpe-page-scale-api.patch: expose visual-viewport (page-scale) zoom.
#  WPE's public API only has webkit_web_view_set_zoom_level(), which is page
#  zoom: it relayouts, reflows text and changes what fits on a line. Pinch on a
#  phone is expected to magnify the rendered page instead, leaving layout alone
#  and letting the user pan around. That is WebCore's page scale factor, which
#  every port has (WebPageProxy::scalePageInViewCoordinates) but no WPE API
#  reaches. Exports wpe_sfos_set_page_scale()/wpe_sfos_get_page_scale() for the
#  embedder's pinch gesture.
#
# ---
Visual-viewport zoom for the WPE GLib API.

`webkit_web_view_set_zoom_level()` maps to WebPageProxy::setPageZoomFactor():
content zoom. The layout viewport is re-resolved at the new factor, so the page
relayouts — text reflows, media queries can flip, and the page never becomes
pannable, it just becomes a differently-laid-out page. That is the right
behaviour for a desktop zoom menu and the wrong behaviour for a pinch gesture,
which users expect to magnify what is already on screen (visual viewport), the
way every other mobile browser behaves.

WebCore already models this separately as the page scale factor
(Page::setPageScaleFactor), reached from the UI process via
WebPageProxy::scalePage()/scalePageInViewCoordinates(). It is cross-platform
(not Cocoa-gated) and composes with page zoom rather than replacing it, which
matters here: the browser uses page zoom 3.0 as its device-scale mechanism, so
pinch must not touch that factor. GTK already keeps the two apart — see the
PLATFORM(GTK) `pageScale` divisor in webkit_web_view_set_zoom_level() — but
under PLATFORM(WPE) that scale is hardwired to 1.0 with no way to set it.

Two exported entry points, mirroring the wpe_sfos_set_dark_mode() convention:

1. wpe_sfos_set_page_scale(view, scale, centerX, centerY) - scale anchored on
   a point in view coordinates, so the content under the fingers stays put.
   scalePageInViewCoordinates() alone does NOT do that: it sets the new scroll
   position to (scrollPosition - origin) * ratio, which anchors the view
   origin, not the centroid. Pinning the centroid needs
   (scrollPosition + center) * ratio - center, and the two agree exactly when
   origin = center * (1/ratio - 1) - independent of the scroll position, so we
   pass that and never have to mirror the scroll offset into the UI process.
   (No in-tree caller to copy from: the only upstream user of this API is
   GTK's GestureController, which the WPE tarball omits.)

2. wpe_sfos_get_page_scale(view) — the UI process needs the committed factor to
   accumulate the next pinch on top of it. Note WebPageProxy resets its cached
   factor to 1 on every main-frame commit, so a new page starts at 1:1 without
   the embedder tracking navigations.

Browser side: apps/wpe/WPEWebPage.cpp drives these from the fallback pinch
gesture (the one used when the page itself does not consume the touches).
--- a/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/API/glib/WebKitWebView.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/API/glib/WebKitWebView.cpp
@@ -5997,3 +5997,52 @@
         xrSystem->invalidate(PlatformXRSystem::InvalidationReason::Client);
 #endif
 }
+
+// SFOS: visual-viewport zoom. The public API only exposes page zoom
+// (webkit_web_view_set_zoom_level -> setPageZoomFactor), which relayouts the
+// page; a pinch gesture wants the page scale factor, which magnifies the
+// rendered result and leaves layout untouched. Both factors compose, so this
+// is independent of the embedder's device-scale page zoom.
+//
+// @scale is absolute (1.0 = unzoomed) and is anchored on (@centerX, @centerY)
+// in view coordinates — the pinch centroid — so the content under the fingers
+// stays under the fingers.
+extern "C" __attribute__((visibility("default")))
+void wpe_sfos_set_page_scale(WebKitWebView* webView, double scale, int centerX, int centerY)
+{
+    g_return_if_fail(WEBKIT_IS_WEB_VIEW(webView));
+
+    if (!(scale > 0))
+        return;
+
+    Ref page = getPage(webView);
+    double currentScale = page->pageScaleFactor();
+    if (!(currentScale > 0) || currentScale == scale)
+        return;
+
+    // scalePageInViewCoordinates() does not pin a view point across a scale
+    // change: it sets the new scroll position to (scrollPosition - origin) *
+    // ratio, which anchors the view origin, not @center. Pinning @center needs
+    // (scrollPosition + center) * ratio - center, and the two agree exactly
+    // when origin = center * (1 / ratio - 1) — for *any* scroll position, so
+    // the scroll offset stays where it is authoritative, in the web process,
+    // and never has to be mirrored up here.
+    //
+    // Exact for a zero scroll origin, i.e. everything except RTL and
+    // vertical-rl documents, where the anchor is off by the scroll origin.
+    double ratio = scale / currentScale;
+    double anchor = 1 / ratio - 1;
+    page->scalePageInViewCoordinates(scale, WebCore::IntPoint(static_cast<int>(centerX * anchor), static_cast<int>(centerY * anchor)));
+}
+
+// SFOS: the page scale factor currently committed for @web_view. The embedder
+// reads this to start a new pinch from the scale the last one left behind.
+// WebPageProxy resets it to 1 on every main-frame commit, so this reports 1.0
+// again after a navigation without the embedder tracking loads.
+extern "C" __attribute__((visibility("default")))
+double wpe_sfos_get_page_scale(WebKitWebView* webView)
+{
+    g_return_val_if_fail(WEBKIT_IS_WEB_VIEW(webView), 1.0);
+
+    return getPage(webView).pageScaleFactor();
+}
